++-++-+++-+-++++",29.239,0.3494],["rs4396807",22,38138379,0.6067,"c","g",6.491,8.52e-11,"TRIOBP","++",0,0.981,"Jawinski et al.","+++?-++?-+?+--?++-+++++++-++",16.203,0.8464]],"notes":["Note. Meta-analysis was conducted in two stages. We first meta-analysed 29 smaller cohorts, each with n < 2,000 (total n =14,413 across all 29 cohorts). Subsequently, these results were combined with the UK Biobank data (n = 46,322) in a second-stage meta-analysis. Genome-wide significance was set at p < 5e-8. SNP independence was defined by linkage disequilibrium pruning with 𝑟2 < 0.1 within 500 kb windows (using 1000 Genomes Phase 3 European reference panel). SNP = single nucleotide polymorphism; chr = chromosome, EAF = effect allele frequency; A1 = effect allele; A2 = non-effect allele; Zscore = Z-statistic for the association of the effect allele with the trait; Direction  = summary of effect direction for each study, with one '+' or '-' per study;  HetISq = I² statistic representing heterogeneity across studies; HetPVal = heterogeneity p-value; Nearest gene = gene closest to the SNP based on genomic position (from Open Targets Platform and dbSNP); proximal non-overlapping genes are shown in brackets.\nLocus reported in prior GWAS = independent significant SNP, or a proxy SNP in linkage disequilibrium with it (r²≥0.1, 1000 Genomes Phase 3 European reference panel; non-GWAS-tagged reference panel SNPs were not included), reported as genome-wide significant (p<5⁻⁸) or suggestive of significance (p<5⁻5) in five previous genome-wide association studies of brain age gap (see Supplementary Methods) ; other studies on brain age gap were not assessed.\nOrder of cohorts in stage 1 meta-analysis  = ACP, BHRCS, BIL&GIN, UNICAMP, COBRE, COMPIMP, DBIS, DCHS, DNS, FBIRN, FIDMAG, GOBS, IGP, imagen, IMH_study, LBC1936, MPRC, PAFIP_3, VOX, SHIP-2, SHIP-T, SHIP-T_B2, GIG, OSLO, SYS, NESDA, i-Share, ALSPAC.\nOrder of cohorts in stage 2 meta-analysis = stage 1 output, UK Biobank."],"glossary":[],"heatmap":null,"group":"gwas"}